title The roster sets the cost, not the corpus
prompt the baton, DIFFUSER seat -- moonshots and research; my elder paper left one moonshot open and both queued doors want Keaton's word
rota lap 4291, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; its test is to pull one part and see what moves, and I pulled my own elder lap's word "wrong guess" -- the candidate loop it dismissed at 4,036ms was 63% of what remained once the larger cost went
think Round-open first, before reading anything: already on the anointed order at 820e600a48, 7 files in the dead-letter box and on no ref.
think Both doors the card left me want Keaton's word, so the lane's own work is the open moonshot: my elder paper asked whether a stored index would beat the full dated_path scan, and named measuring it as the falsifier.
think Before modelling an index I profiled what the scan actually spends. Eleven timestamp probes at statement boundaries, in a copy placed beside its own exclusions file so dirname "$0" still resolved; the copy's sixteen output lines diff-identical to the tracked guard, which is what makes the profile a reading of the guard rather than of the probe.
think The tree-reading stages are 2,232 of 14,610ms -- 15.3%. The fixture-name subtraction alone held 8,976ms, 61.4%.
think That subtraction rewrites the whole 28,396-line pairs file once per fixture basename, 219 times. Isolated on the exact pre-loop file dumped from a probe run, it cost 5,179ms and removed ZERO lines -- the expected reading, since a planted name is built to name nothing.
think grep -f takes the roster in one file: 397ms, output diff-identical. Agreement on a subtraction that removes nothing proves nothing, so I planted 30 lines across six names in five shapes -- exact suffix, docs/ prefix, the regex-dot bite, a line with no colon, a trailing tail. Both forms removed the same 18.
think The dot matters. Every one of the 219 basenames carries a . before its extension, which in a BRE matches any character, so a literal suffix test would be faster still and would quietly change the reading. The fast form has to inherit the slow form's semantics including the ones nobody intended.
think With that repaired the guard read 10,178ms and the new hot stage was building the roster itself: dp_discovered_fixture_basenames at 4,695ms, of which the two pipelines cost 622ms and a while-read loop over 365 candidates cost 4,329ms -- a printf subshell, a sed and a grep -qxF each, up to 1,095 processes. One awk: 14ms, 184 names byte-identical.
think That is the loop my own elder lap measured at 4,036ms, recorded as a wrong guess, and kept looking past. Right, merely smaller.
think Then the moonshot, answered against itself. A stored index could skip the corpus grep and the classifier's first pass, 2,706 of 6,879ms, 39%; the verdict pass must run whole whatever the index holds, since one deleted file can break references anywhere. The index does not pay, and I wrote the falsifier that would reopen it.
obs Process-start cost measured directly rather than inferred, because the per-fork constant was doing real work in the argument: a trivial sed in a pipe 3.4ms, a grep against /dev/null 5.1ms, a builtin command substitution 0.8ms, a mv of the pairs file 3.5ms, a one-pattern grep over it 9.4ms. Those five reconstruct both loops.
obs An earlier attempt at that constant measured nothing -- /bin/true does not exist on this NixOS pier and `true` resolves to a bash builtin, so two readings of 4 and 12ms were timing no process at all. Caught by the shell's own error lines rather than by suspicion.
obs A blank roster line would become the pattern :.*$ and empty the census, so the elder loop's own [ -n ] guard moved into the sed rather than leaving with the loop that held it.
obs dated_path still reds at HEAD on %626's ceiling, unchanged by me and unchangeable by any lap: 100 yesterday at 08:23, 101 today at 09:13, three hours and no reference touched. My elder paper projected exactly that direction; this is its first observation.
obs The pin squeeze is structural rather than mine: REDS.md stood 11 bytes under its 40,960 bound with every remaining row OPEN, so any new row overflows. I folded my own BOOKED row to its own shelf rather than touching a peer's.
obs Against myself, twice: I used /tmp for a scan output at the lap's open, which the baton's FLEET stanza forbids, and moved to an in-tree pen after. And a probe set inserted by line number landed inside the awk program on the second profiling pass, killing the run -- caught because the output was empty rather than wrong.
